WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.780
<v Maya>Hi everyone, and welcome to the Fitness Testing series.

2
00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:05.000
<v Maya>I'm Maya.

3
00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:06.350
<v Dev>And I'm Dev.

4
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:14.290
<v Dev>This series is for anyone doing a personal training course who wants to really understand fitness testing, just by listening.

5
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:15.580
<v Maya>That's the idea.

6
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:22.420
<v Maya>Put the notes away, and by the end you should be able to explain every test, every number and every reason behind the order.

7
00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.270
<v Maya>Not just memorise it.

8
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:29.140
<v Dev>Good, because tables of norms are exactly what I forget the next morning.

9
00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.470
<v Maya>We'll avoid that.

10
00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:35.300
<v Maya>We explain, Dev asks the questions a student would ask, and we stop for recaps.

11
00:00:35.350 --> 00:00:36.750
<v Maya>There are eight parts.

12
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:42.920
<v Maya>Four cover the first lecture, Fitness Testing One, and four cover the second lecture, Fitness Testing Two.

13
00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:44.940
<v Dev>So what's in the first four?

14
00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:52.850
<v Maya>Part one, today: why we test at all, the objectives of testing, and health screening with the PARQ and the informed consent form.

15
00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:57.110
<v Maya>Part two: the correct order of the tests, and the first test, blood pressure.

16
00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:04.130
<v Maya>Part three: body composition, starting with the body measurements, waist circumference, waist to hip ratio and BMI.

17
00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:11.100
<v Maya>Part four: skinfold calipers, the BIA machine and the Bod Pod, and a comparison of everything so far.

18
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.720
<v Dev>And lecture two?

19
00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:19.710
<v Maya>Part five: underwater weighing, the DEXA scan, and how we choose the best of the eight body composition tests.

20
00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:24.960
<v Maya>Part six: the heart and lung tests, the Rockport walk and the YMCA step test.

21
00:01:25.010 --> 00:01:28.930
<v Maya>Part seven: the strength tests and the muscular endurance tests.

22
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.660
<v Maya>Part eight: the flexibility tests, a full summary, and a twelve question quiz on the whole topic.

23
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.280
<v Dev>That's the whole journey.

24
00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:37.540
<v Dev>Okay, let's start.

25
00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.060
<v Maya>Let's start with a simple question, Dev.

26
00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:42.700
<v Maya>Fitness testing is taught in two parts.

27
00:01:42.750 --> 00:01:45.210
<v Maya>What do you think these tests have in common?

28
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:47.740
<v Dev>They all measure how fit someone is?

29
00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:49.460
<v Maya>Yes, and one more thing.

30
00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:50.990
<v Maya>They are standardised.

31
00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:58.230
<v Maya>That means they are conducted the same way all over the world, with the same guidelines, procedures, methods and test norms.

32
00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.540
<v Dev>Test norms.

33
00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:01.080
<v Dev>What are those?

34
00:02:01.130 --> 00:02:03.250
<v Maya>A test norm is a reference sheet.

35
00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.580
<v Maya>After a test you get a number or a score, and you check it against the norm sheet.

36
00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:11.900
<v Maya>The sheet shows where that person stands for the fitness component you tested.

37
00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:14.100
<v Dev>So the number alone means nothing.

38
00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:16.740
<v Dev>The sheet tells you if it's good or bad.

39
00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:17.880
<v Maya>Exactly.

40
00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:20.080
<v Maya>And fitness has five components.

41
00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:26.360
<v Maya>Cardiorespiratory endurance, muscular strength, muscular endurance, flexibility and body composition.

42
00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:29.440
<v Maya>So you cannot call someone fit from one or two tests.

43
00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.620
<v Maya>You must test all the components.

44
00:02:31.670 --> 00:02:33.540
<v Dev>And who made up these tests?

45
00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:34.830
<v Dev>The course?

46
00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.670
<v Maya>No.

47
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:42.420
<v Maya>They were developed through research by international bodies in fitness science, and they are accepted and used worldwide.

48
00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.420
<v Dev>Why do we test at all, though?

49
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:47.120
<v Dev>Can't a trainer just look at a client?

50
00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:52.180
<v Maya>Testing just means evaluating something to check its performance, quality or efficacy.

51
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.700
<v Maya>Doctors test to diagnose disease.

52
00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:57.090
<v Maya>Companies test a product before launch.

53
00:02:57.140 --> 00:02:59.120
<v Maya>Software gets tested heavily.

54
00:02:59.170 --> 00:03:00.770
<v Maya>Exams test knowledge.

55
00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:06.540
<v Maya>Fitness testing is the same idea, evaluating a person's fitness with defined objectives.

56
00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:09.120
<v Maya>The objectives come from two perspectives.

57
00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.140
<v Maya>The global perspective and the client perspective.

58
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.490
<v Maya>Global first.

59
00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:17.670
<v Maya>There are two objectives, standardisation and performance benchmarking.

60
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:19.460
<v Dev>Standardisation I get.

61
00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:21.300
<v Dev>Same test everywhere.

62
00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:22.200
<v Maya>Right.

63
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:29.290
<v Maya>Muscular strength is not tested with a lat pull down in one country, a shoulder press in another and a bench press in a third.

64
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:34.720
<v Maya>One of the worldwide strength tests is the bench press, and nobody may invent a different method.

65
00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.780
<v Maya>That is what allows comparison.

66
00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:42.450
<v Maya>Athletes or bodybuilders of one country can be compared with those of another on the same measure.

67
00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:44.060
<v Dev>And benchmarking?

68
00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:47.850
<v Maya>Imagine a country's athletes are going to a global tournament.

69
00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:52.160
<v Maya>If they don't know where the competition stands, they have no milestone to aim for.

70
00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:56.160
<v Maya>Benchmarking shows how far a team lags, or how far ahead it is.

71
00:03:56.210 --> 00:03:58.120
<v Dev>Why would being ahead matter?

72
00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.890
<v Maya>Because then you know the standard must be maintained.

73
00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:07.120
<v Maya>So globally, fitness testing is about performance benchmarking, made possible by standardised methods.

74
00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.300
<v Dev>Okay, now the client side.

75
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:10.790
<v Maya>Four objectives.

76
00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:13.700
<v Maya>The lecture links them to a simple business truth.

77
00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:16.520
<v Maya>If testing helps the client, it helps the trainer.

78
00:04:16.570 --> 00:04:19.590
<v Maya>A happy client stays, renews, and tells others.

79
00:04:19.640 --> 00:04:21.610
<v Maya>That is a brand advocate.

80
00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.470
<v Dev>So, four objectives.

81
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:24.540
<v Dev>Go.

82
00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:26.350
<v Maya>One, baseline results.

83
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.660
<v Maya>The first test.

84
00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:34.110
<v Maya>The trainer knows nothing about a new client, so testing all five components shows where the client stands today.

85
00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:35.830
<v Maya>Two, progress tracking.

86
00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:40.010
<v Maya>After, say, three months, a re-test shows how far the client has come.

87
00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:43.750
<v Maya>You can say, this is where you started and this is where we have brought you.

88
00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:46.030
<v Maya>Numbers speak louder than words.

89
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.420
<v Dev>And it shows the trainer isn't just promising magic.

90
00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:50.310
<v Maya>Yes.

91
00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:56.990
<v Maya>The notes say a trainer who is number driven and goal oriented, not someone saying, give me one year, I will do magic.

92
00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.500
<v Maya>Three, goal setting.

93
00:04:58.550 --> 00:05:07.860
<v Maya>Baseline and re-test results together let you set short term, mid term and long term goals, with real milestones, for example at three months and six months.

94
00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:12.280
<v Maya>After a re-test you can tweak the goals if the plan is or isn't working.

95
00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.550
<v Dev>And four?

96
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:15.640
<v Maya>Strengths and areas to work on.

97
00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:21.970
<v Maya>The baseline shows what the client is good at and what needs work, so you can design a customised workout plan.

98
00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.140
<v Dev>Why not just say weaknesses?

99
00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:26.310
<v Maya>Fitness is a service industry.

100
00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:31.040
<v Maya>A client meeting you for the first time may react badly to a list of weaknesses.

101
00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:33.640
<v Maya>So we say strengths and areas to work on.

102
00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:37.020
<v Maya>Also, strength here is the ordinary English word.

103
00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.150
<v Maya>It can come from any of the five components, not only muscular strength.

104
00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:43.240
<v Dev>Is there a way to picture that?

105
00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:45.340
<v Maya>The lecture uses a school debate.

106
00:05:45.390 --> 00:05:48.150
<v Maya>A girl takes part, and her teacher gives feedback.

107
00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.080
<v Maya>The weaknesses are pronunciation and grammar.

108
00:05:51.130 --> 00:05:53.870
<v Maya>The strength is excellent voice modulation.

109
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:55.860
<v Maya>Strengths can lie in any field.

110
00:05:55.910 --> 00:05:57.440
<v Maya>So how do you use them?

111
00:05:57.490 --> 00:06:04.120
<v Maya>A client with very good cardiorespiratory endurance can be helped to maximise that, even build a career from it.

112
00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:09.360
<v Maya>A client with very good upper body strength gets more advanced upper body exercises.

113
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.390
<v Dev>And the areas to work on?

114
00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:12.680
<v Maya>Two examples.

115
00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:18.270
<v Maya>A client with poor flexibility gets a plan where not a single day passes without stretching.

116
00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.200
<v Maya>Mobility drills as well as static stretching.

117
00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:23.960
<v Maya>And a client with a contraindication.

118
00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:25.420
<v Dev>Contra what?

119
00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:30.000
<v Maya>A contraindication is any movement that causes discomfort or pain.

120
00:06:30.050 --> 00:06:32.440
<v Maya>Physiotherapists use the word a lot.

121
00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:35.270
<v Maya>It might be in the upper limb, lower limb or back.

122
00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.290
<v Maya>For example, raising the hand with the elbow bent.

123
00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:44.380
<v Maya>If a machine or exercise bothers the client, you choose an alternative, and you avoid injury risk.

124
00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:45.710
<v Dev>Let me recap.

125
00:06:45.760 --> 00:06:48.610
<v Dev>Globally, standardisation and benchmarking.

126
00:06:48.660 --> 00:06:54.610
<v Dev>For the client, baseline, progress tracking, goal setting, and strengths and areas to work on.

127
00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:55.630
<v Maya>Perfect.

128
00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:57.650
<v Maya>That's the whole objectives tree.

129
00:06:57.700 --> 00:06:58.860
<v Maya>Pause and think.

130
00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.670
<v Maya>Why does a trainer need baseline results before setting goals?

131
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:08.050
<v Dev>Because without a starting point the goals would be random, and you could never show progress.

132
00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.210
<v Maya>Exactly right.

133
00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.930
<v Maya>Now, before any test, there is a pre-step.

134
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:13.150
<v Maya>Health screening.

135
00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.360
<v Maya>Dev, if I say screening, what comes to mind?

136
00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.390
<v Dev>Airport baggage scanners.

137
00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:20.550
<v Maya>That is the best way to think of it.

138
00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:27.040
<v Maya>At airports, metro stations and hotels, security scans luggage so people in a public place are safe.

139
00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:33.540
<v Maya>Health screening makes sure the person you are about to test is safe and sound, and nothing goes wrong during the test.

140
00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.880
<v Dev>Is there a formal definition?

141
00:07:35.930 --> 00:07:43.710
<v Maya>The course slides say, a procedure performed on asymptomatic populations to assess the likelihood of a particular disease.

142
00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:46.400
<v Maya>Asymptomatic means without symptoms.

143
00:07:46.450 --> 00:07:49.220
<v Dev>Do I need a doctor at the gym for this?

144
00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:50.060
<v Maya>No.

145
00:07:50.110 --> 00:07:52.550
<v Maya>Health screening uses two simple forms.

146
00:07:52.600 --> 00:07:57.390
<v Maya>The trainer reads the form to the client, or the client reads it, fills it in and signs it.

147
00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:01.370
<v Maya>The two forms are the PARQ and the informed consent form.

148
00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:06.430
<v Maya>They are also a requirement of the international registration body, so you must know them.

149
00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.460
<v Maya>PARQ stands for Physical Activity Readiness Questionnaire.

150
00:08:10.510 --> 00:08:13.680
<v Maya>About ten questions on the client's medical background.

151
00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:18.600
<v Maya>Heart conditions, joint problems, neurological conditions like epilepsy.

152
00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:21.370
<v Maya>The sample in the slides has ten questions.

153
00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:23.140
<v Dev>What kind of questions?

154
00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:24.310
<v Maya>Close ended.

155
00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:25.750
<v Maya>Only yes or no.

156
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.010
<v Maya>No essays.

157
00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.560
<v Maya>For example, has your doctor ever said you have a heart condition?

158
00:08:30.610 --> 00:08:32.810
<v Maya>Is there heart disease in your family?

159
00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:37.370
<v Maya>Do you feel chest pain during activity, or in the past month when not active?

160
00:08:37.420 --> 00:08:40.940
<v Maya>Do you lose balance because of dizziness or lose consciousness?

161
00:08:40.990 --> 00:08:44.170
<v Maya>Do you have a bone or joint problem that could get worse?

162
00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:48.430
<v Maya>Do you have asthma, diabetes, epilepsy or high blood pressure?

163
00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.960
<v Maya>Any other medical condition, any current injury, or any other reason you should not exercise?

164
00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:55.960
<v Dev>And what counts as passing?

165
00:08:56.010 --> 00:08:59.490
<v Maya>The PARQ is cleared only when all answers are no.

166
00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:02.470
<v Maya>A single yes is a signal to stop and ask.

167
00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.900
<v Maya>To be sure the client is eligible, they must bring a NOC from their doctor.

168
00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.860
<v Maya>That is a no objection certificate, the doctor's written clearance.

169
00:09:10.910 --> 00:09:13.170
<v Dev>Why so strict about forms?

170
00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:14.600
<v Maya>Documentation.

171
00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:18.900
<v Maya>Today a client can blame a trainer and threaten legal action very quickly.

172
00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:24.880
<v Maya>If everything is documented and signed before the journey starts, nobody can hold you responsible later.

173
00:09:24.930 --> 00:09:26.880
<v Dev>And the informed consent?

174
00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:36.740
<v Maya>Informed means there is a script that tells the client about the machines that will be used, the exercises, such as strength training, cardio and stretching, and the possible risks.

175
00:09:36.790 --> 00:09:39.840
<v Maya>It covers the whole fitness journey, not only the test.

176
00:09:39.890 --> 00:09:47.470
<v Maya>Then the consent part is the closing statement, where the client says they are fully aware and willingly agree, followed by a signature.

177
00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:48.840
<v Dev>So a win-win.

178
00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:52.010
<v Dev>The client is aware, the trainer is protected.

179
00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:52.900
<v Maya>Yes.

180
00:09:52.950 --> 00:10:05.810
<v Maya>The sample also says it is the client's responsibility to tell the trainer about health problems, injuries or pregnancy, to get medical clearance if needed, and to stop and inform the trainer if unusual symptoms occur.

181
00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.050
<v Dev>What if my gym already has its own forms?

182
00:10:09.100 --> 00:10:10.170
<v Maya>Follow them.

183
00:10:10.220 --> 00:10:11.900
<v Maya>Don't go against the system.

184
00:10:11.950 --> 00:10:16.330
<v Maya>If the gym has none, introduce one, and it will be seen as a positive step.

185
00:10:16.380 --> 00:10:17.540
<v Maya>Pause and think.

186
00:10:17.590 --> 00:10:21.890
<v Maya>A client answers yes to the question about chest pain during activity.

187
00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:23.200
<v Maya>What do you do?

188
00:10:23.250 --> 00:10:29.390
<v Dev>Stop, ask about it, and don't test until they bring a no objection certificate from their doctor.

189
00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:30.200
<v Maya>Spot on.

190
00:10:30.250 --> 00:10:31.770
<v Maya>Let's close part one.

191
00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:36.020
<v Maya>We covered what testing is, the objectives, and the pre-step of screening.

192
00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:37.550
<v Dev>Here's my version.

193
00:10:37.600 --> 00:10:41.870
<v Dev>Fitness testing is standardised and compared against test norms.

194
00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:45.140
<v Dev>Globally it gives standardisation and benchmarking.

195
00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:50.910
<v Dev>For the client it gives baseline, progress tracking, goals, and strengths and areas to work on.

196
00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:55.340
<v Dev>Before testing, screen with the PARQ and informed consent.

197
00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:57.350
<v Maya>Now the five things to remember.

198
00:10:57.400 --> 00:11:01.260
<v Maya>One, tests are standardised, and a test norm is a reference sheet.

199
00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:04.830
<v Maya>Two, there are six objectives, two global and four client.

200
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:07.990
<v Maya>Three, say areas to work on, not weaknesses.

201
00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:13.900
<v Maya>Four, the PARQ is cleared only by all no answers, otherwise you need a doctor's NOC.

202
00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:20.150
<v Maya>Five, the informed consent script covers machines, exercises and risks, and ends with a signature.

203
00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:23.460
<v Dev>Next time, the order of testing, and blood pressure.

204
00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:24.280
<v Maya>See you then.

